# 5. List Indexing

Every element in a list has an index.

Python indexing starts from `0`.

Example:

```python
fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Orange", "Mango"]
```

Index positions:

```text
Apple   -> 0
Banana  -> 1
Orange  -> 2
Mango   -> 3
```

### Accessing Elements

```python
fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Orange", "Mango"]

print(fruits[0])
print(fruits[1])
print(fruits[2])
```

Output:

```text
Apple
Banana
Orange
```

---

# 6. Negative Indexing

Python also supports negative indexing.

The last element has index `-1`.

```python
fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Orange", "Mango"]

print(fruits[-1])
print(fruits[-2])
```

Output:

```text
Mango
Orange
```

Index structure:

```text
Apple   Banana   Orange   Mango
  0       1        2        3
 -4      -3       -2       -1
```

### Real-World Example

Getting the latest item from a list:

```python
notifications = [
    "New message",
    "Friend request",
    "System update"
]

print(notifications[-1])
```

